  "worked_solution": "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 100 \\\\ {x}^{2}-100 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-100\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{10}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-10)(x+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 10.$ \n\nSolving the inequality ${x}^{2}-100\\ge 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-100$ is above or intersects the $x$-axis (where $y\\ge 0$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (including them), as shown below.\n\n![](@asset-0@)\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-10]\\cup [10,\\infty ).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
